<section class="band band--w" id="story"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="shead">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-rv>How it started</p>
      <h2 class="h2" data-rv style="--d:60ms">The question nobody had a column for.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="dek" data-rv style="--d:100ms">Akshata spent years watching capable teams run sensible
    processes and still get it wrong. <b>Not on credentials, those were nearly always fine.</b></p>
  </div>
  <div class="story" data-rv style="--d:140ms">
    <div>
      <p class="story__p">The shortlist was strong. The interviews were structured. Everyone in the room
      was experienced. And a year later roughly a third of those hires had quietly not worked, for reasons
      nobody had written down and nobody could point to afterwards.</p>
      <p class="story__p">The thing being missed was never on the scorecard, because no scorecard had a
      column for it.</p>
    </div>
    <blockquote class="story__q">Who will actually<br><em>belong here?</em></blockquote>
  </div>
  <p class="story__ft" data-rv>Two of us sat down over a coffee to work out whether that question could be
  answered rather than felt. <b>It took two hours and the coffee went cold.</b> Pranav joined a few weeks
  later. <b>Between the three of us we left two salaries, a consulting practice and a board seat</b>, and
  told our families it would take a year before there was anything to show them.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="fn band" id="whynow"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="shead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr;text-align:center;justify-items:center;gap:8px;margin-bottom:clamp(16px,2vw,24px)">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-rv>Why now</p>
      <h2 class="h2" data-rv style="--d:60ms;max-width:none;text-align:center">More reach.<br>Less signal.</h2></div>
    <p class="fn__dek" data-rv style="--d:100ms;max-width:none;text-align:center">Reaching a candidate has become easier. Deciding who will
    belong has not. Those two things are connected, and the industry has not yet said so out loud.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wn" data-rv style="--d:140ms">
    <div class="wn__g"><svg class="wn__c" viewBox="0 0 460 168" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Hires per recruiter per quarter, 2021 to 2025"><line x1="26" y1="126.3" x2="434" y2="126.3" stroke="#2E2E2C" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="26" y1="95.0" x2="434" y2="95.0" stroke="#2E2E2C" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="26" y1="63.6" x2="434" y2="63.6" stroke="#2E2E2C" stroke-width="1"/><line x1="26" y1="32.3" x2="434" y2="32.3" stroke="#2E2E2C" stroke-width="1"/><text x="6" y="129.3" fill="#6E6E68" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9">4</text><text x="6" y="98.0" fill="#6E6E68" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9">5</text><text x="6" y="66.6" fill="#6E6E68" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9">6</text><text x="6" y="35.3" fill="#6E6E68" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9">7</text><text x="26.0" y="160" fill="#6E6E68" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">2021</text><text x="230.0" y="160" fill="#6E6E68" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">2023</text><text x="434.0" y="160" fill="#6E6E68" font-family="JetBrains Mono, monospace" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">2025</text><path class="wn__p wn__p--q" pathLength="1" d="M26.0 35.4 L77.0 51.1 L128.0 69.9 L179.0 95.0 L230.0 116.9 L281.0 107.5 L332.0 91.8 L383.0 82.4 L434.0 82.4" stroke="#DA0007" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" fill="none"/><circle cx="26.0" cy="35.4" r="4" fill="#8E8A80"/><circle cx="230.0" cy="116.9" r="4.5" fill="#DA0007"/><circle cx="434.0" cy="82.4" r="4" fill="#8E8A80"/></svg>
      <p class="wn__cap">Hires per recruiter, per quarter. <b>Nearly seven in early 2021, a low of about
      four by 2023, and roughly five and a half since</b>, under two hires a month.</p>
      <p class="fn__line" data-rv>Everyone automated the finding.<br>We built for <em>the deciding</em>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wn__t">
      <div class="wn__s"><span class="wn__n">01</span>
        <p class="wn__st">Sourcing became almost free</p>
        <p class="wn__sx">One person with the right tools can put four hundred profiles in front of a
        hiring manager in an afternoon. That took a team a month not long ago.</p></div>
      <div class="wn__s"><span class="wn__n">02</span>
        <p class="wn__st">So the pipeline stopped meaning anything</p>
        <p class="wn__sx">Applications per hire are up around <b>180%</b> on 2021. Requisitions per
        recruiter up by half again. Interviews per hire up <b>40%</b>. Every one of those is a number
        going the wrong way while looking like progress.</p></div>
      <div class="wn__s wn__s--end"><span class="wn__n">03</span>
        <p class="wn__st">Which made judgment the scarce thing</p>
        <p class="wn__sx">Somebody senior, who has done the job, deciding what a specific answer was
        worth. <b>That has not got cheaper, and it does not scale by buying another tool.</b></p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="cand band" id="letter"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="ltr">
    <p class="ltr__k">From Akshata</p>
    <div class="ltr__cols">
      <div class="ltr__col ltr__col--a">
        <p class="ltr__lede">The pause was always the same.</p>
        <p class="ltr__sub">A profile reads well, until someone asks a real question about the work.</p>
        <p>Twenty years in, the scoreboard had never changed. Number of recruiters. Number of clients.
      Number of CVs sent. Every target I was given measured effort, and every target I handed down did the
      same. None of them measured whether we were right. I did not question it for a long time,
      because nobody did.</p>
        <p>And you can see what that produced. Every year there are more tools, more automation, and more
      profiles reaching more people. Activity looks like progress until someone has to decide whether a
      person will actually do the work. That is what happens when activity is the only thing anyone counts.</p>
        <p>You know the moment I mean. A profile goes across that reads well, the client brings the person
      in, and somebody asks a real question about the work: what they decided, what happened when it went
      wrong. Then the pause. I have been the person in that pause, on both sides of the table. It
      was never that the recruiter was not good. We sent them in with a CV, an instinct and hope, and
      called it a process.</p>
        <blockquote class="ltr__q">Everybody in that room deserved<br>an answer. Nobody had been given one.</blockquote>
        <p>Because the tools were never made. Nothing to help a recruiter read a job description in a field
      they have never worked in. Nothing that turns what a hiring manager means by "strong" into something
      you can check a person against. Other industries built instruments. We hired more people and sent
      more CVs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="ltr__col ltr__col--b">
        <p>I did not start as a recruiter. I learnt it, and somewhere in twenty years of doing it hands-on
      it stopped being work. I read a CV, I talk to the person about the technology and the domain, and I
      know: not a hunch to be checked later, I know. I can count the times I have been wrong about
      someone I put forward, and I do not need many fingers.</p>
        <p>And it was the least useful talent imaginable. Because it lived in one head. I could sit with the recruiter next to me and talk them through a profile, and by the end of the call they would see what I saw. They just could not do it again on their own the following week. It scaled to exactly as many searches as I could personally sit in, which against what this industry runs in a week is nothing at all.</p>
        <p>For years I said all of this out loud, to anyone who would sit still for it. Almost everyone
      agreed. Almost nobody could act on it, not because they did not care, but because the pressure
      to keep delivering numbers never lets up long enough to stop and fix the thing producing them.
      I had lived inside that. Eventually I stopped waiting, and left.</p>
        <p>Then Abhishek said he could build the part that transfers. And he did. The hundred signals
      read before anyone picks up a phone, the questions written off a candidate’s own CV, the scoring
      against a bar agreed in advance, that runs now at a scale I never could.</p>
        <p>The rest does not transfer, and we stopped pretending it would.</p>
<p>Reading a person is not a machine problem. This is a people industry, entirely, so the
        engine hands its work to a senior expert who has done that job, and they do the part no model gets
        to do. Fifty of them. Fifty heads instead of one.</p>
        <p>So this is the instrument, and it is built for the recruiter. That half hour with a candidate is
      the most valuable thing in this business, and everything we have made exists to protect it, so
      the conversation gets to be about the person instead of the logistics.</p>
        <p>I still run searches myself, full-time, with my name on the outcome. Not because the company needs
      me to. Because the day I stop doing the job is the day I stop being any use to the people
      building the thing that does it.</p>
        <p>This industry gave me a career before I had earned one. <b>This is what I owe it back.</b></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ltr__sign">
      <span class="sig" aria-label="Akshata Chandavarkar">Akshata Chandavarkar</span>
      <svg class="sig__f" viewBox="0 0 300 22" fill="none" preserveAspectRatio="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M296 4 Q300 12 286 16 Q210 22 130 19 Q54 16 14 12 Q2 9 10 4" stroke="#DA0007" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" fill="none" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/></svg>
      <p class="ltr__ro">Founder &amp; CEO, tribera</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="band" id="team"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="shead">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--red" data-rv>The three of us</p>
      <h2 class="h2" data-rv style="--d:60ms">Built by people who were in the room.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="dek" data-rv style="--d:100ms">Not observers of this industry. <b>We ran the searches, made
    the calls, and carried the ones that went wrong.</b></p>
  </div>
  <div class="fnds" data-rv style="--d:140ms"><div class="fnd"><span class="fnd__ph" id="ph-akshata" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="fnd__n">Akshata Chandavarkar</p><p class="fnd__r">Founder &amp; CEO</p><p class="fnd__x">Twenty years of enterprise hiring across GCCs, BFSI, product and deep-tech. Ran talent acquisition at GCC scale, and still runs live searches herself, on the bar the engine is built on: the one she works to.</p><p class="fnd__q">She had asked the question for years before anyone tried to answer it.</p></div></div><div class="fnd"><span class="fnd__ph" id="ph-abhishek" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="fnd__n">Abhishek Prasad</p><p class="fnd__r">Co-founder &amp; CBO</p><p class="fnd__x">Two decades building products used by millions. Architected the signal engine: what a CV hides, join probability, offer behaviour, retention. The hundred-plus signals behind every verdict are his.</p><p class="fnd__q">He left that coffee unable to stop seeing the problem.</p></div></div><div class="fnd"><span class="fnd__ph" id="ph-pranav" aria-hidden="true"></span><div><p class="fnd__n">Pranav Gandhi</p><p class="fnd__r">Chief Success Officer</p><p class="fnd__x">Twenty years scaling companies past five hundred people, and living the arithmetic of it: how one wrong senior hire quietly resets a culture that took three years to build. Runs client delivery and the quality bar behind every mandate.</p><p class="fnd__q">He had watched a good hire cost more than a bad quarter.</p></div></div></div>
</div></section>

<section class="fn band" id="year"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="shead">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow" data-rv>What we did about it</p>
      <h2 class="h2" data-rv style="--d:60ms">We spent a year not launching.</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="fn__dek" data-rv style="--d:100ms">The obvious move was to ship something small and learn in
    public. <b>We did the opposite, because the thing being tested was whether a stranger’s judgment could
    be trusted with someone’s career</b>, and you can’t iterate your way through that in front of real
    candidates.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="jrny" data-rv style="--d:140ms"><div class="jstep"><span class="jstep__bar"></span><svg class="hdraw" viewBox="0 0 92 62" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true"><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M14.0 30.0 Q14.9 36.7 14.7 45.6 Q18.2 49.5 20.5 51.4 Q25.8 50.7 31.6 52.0 Q33.2 48.2 37.2 46.1 Q36.7 38.2 38.4 29.3" stroke="#B0ACA2" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity="1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M14.0 30.0 Q14.9 36.7 14.7 45.6 Q18.2 49.5 20.5 51.4 Q25.8 50.7 31.6 52.0 Q33.2 48.2 37.2 46.1 Q36.7 38.2 38.4 29.3" stroke="#B0ACA2" stroke-width="1.19" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity="0.38" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M12.0 29.0 Q19.2 28.5 24.8 26.5 Q33.6 27.6 39.4 28.4" stroke="#B0ACA2" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity="1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M12.0 29.0 Q19.2 28.5 24.8 26.5 Q33.6 27.6 39.4 28.4" stroke="#B0ACA2" stroke-width="1.19" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity="0.38" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M52.0 30.0 Q53.5 38.3 53.4 46.3 Q55.6 50.3 57.8 52.1 Q64.9 52.3 70.5 52.1 Q73.1 47.7 74.6 45.7 Q74.3 37.3 75.4 29.7" stroke="#B0ACA2" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity="1" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M52.0 30.0 Q53.5 38.3 53.4 46.3 Q55.6 50.3 57.8 52.1 Q64.9 52.3 70.5 52.1 Q73.1 47.7 74.6 45.7 Q74.3 37.3 75.4 29.7" stroke="#B0ACA2" stroke-width="1.19" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity="0.38" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"/><path class="hd" pathLength="1" d="M50.0 29.0 Q56.8 27.7 62.8 26.6 Q69.9 29.0 78.2 29.2" stroke="#B0ACA2" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" opacity="1" vector-effect="non-scaling-str